It was final inning for Bob Ingram by Bill Thompson 
It was just a small, late afternoon gathering, a little cocktail party on the top floor of a downtown skyscraper, nobody much there except the
newsroom and sports staff of The Herald-Post, who had come to say good-bye to an old friend
We all had a few drinks, munched on those things made to munch on at cocktail parties and gathered in our little cliques and talked shop
THE MAN WE came to say goodbye to was just finishing up over half a century as the first and only sports editor of The Herald-Post, a longer
span than most of the people in the room had lived
Somewhere in the middle of the party we gave him a silver belt buckle with the name Bob Ingram, engraved on the back and he said thanks and no
more, Bob was never much for making speeches
A few old friends were there, Sid and Andy Cohen, the old major leagZuers and some others I didn't know;
Jim Paul of the Diablos (baseball was Bob's favorite sport), Chuck Whitlock and John Phelan, I sat and watched the little party and wondered if all of
those people knew just how much Bob Ingram had contributed to sports in 54 years. If all of the people he had written about and all of the people whom
he had touched with his words down through the years had come, the Sun Bow! wouldn't have held them
THE WORLD OF sports has always represented a lot of things that we consider good, the spirit of fair play and competition, the comradeship of
teamwork. Never mind that it 1s sometimes a little tawdry and it has its share of heroes with clay feet, the heroes of sports. The real heroes are
still people we look up to,
Bob Ingram wrote about those people for over half a century with a singleness of purpose that was sometimes disconcerting. Many a Sunday morning I used to come down to the dar
kened newspaper offices and there was Bob, at his desk, writing a column, polishing it until the words shone like jewels

