The joke was on Cohen: He could have worn pinstripes By Bob Ingram  Former Herald-Post sports editor
EDITOR'S NOTE: Bob Ingram, former sports editor of the Herald-Post, was asked to write about his experiences with Andy and Syd Cohen, who
are being honored with the construction of a sports oes, Persons wishing to contribute to the campaign may mail donations to the Andy and Syd
Cohen Sports Complex, P.O. Box 12097, E! Paso 79913.
‘There has been no legal process of it, but I became a brother of Andy and Sydney Cohen.
T was “adopted” by their mother, Mrs, Lena Cohen, during the baseball peringrindations of her two sons, They wrote home quite often, but she
still wanted more information on their diamond exploits. She called me frequently at my sports desk. I was glad to help and she was so grateful she
started calling me “Son Bob." I was real flattered.
I first met Andy when he returned from his first season with the New York Giants. The Giants bought him from the Waco club of the Texas League
during the season for a reported 25000, And, incidentally, he might have worn the New York Yankee pinstripes had he taken a telegram from the Yankees at face value.. They told him they were interested in him.  It was the day of the pratical joke in El Paso. Andy had
seen other players dupede So he didn’t answer the Yankees until it was too late.
I was at the Cohen home in El Paso a good deal andI watched with admiration the close ties between the mother and her two sons. If either one left the house, they were hugged and kissed by their mother.
‘Andy was a fine looking athlete when he came back from his seasons with the Giants. He had that friendly and warm personality that he has today.  One of the things he did when he returned from the Giants was to build his mother a fine
home on N. Stanton St.
Andy's biggest thrill as a player? It was the opening day of the season in the Polo Grounds in 1928. Andy was breaking in as the Giants’ second baseman again the Boston Braves. Andy hit two singles  With the game coming down to the nitty gritty, the
Boston strategy was to walk George Harper of the Giants to get to the rookie Cohen. Andy lashed out a double that won the game 5-4. There was tumultuous applause and the fans came out of the stands and carried the rookie off the field.  John McGraw had at last found
the player who appealed to the large Jewish population of New York City. Biographical stories of Cohen ran in newspapers for days. 

Andy hit .294 one season but that still wasn't enough. You see, he had to compete against the great Rogers Hornsby. “Nobody was beating him out of a job,” Andy said.
Tt was off to Buffalo and Newark for young Cohen. Andy was having such a great season with the latter that McGraw personally came to Newark to bring him back to the Giants. ,
With McGraw in the stands, Andy suffered one of his greatest misfortunes. He broke a leg sliding into a base. His opportunity to return to the big leagues was doomed. But he went on to have some big seasons with Minneapolis in the American Assocation and became protege and confidant of
club owner Mike Kelley.

Then it was on to Pine Bluff, Ark.; Dayton, Ohio; Elmira, N.Y.; Eau Claire, Wis., and finally to the ill-fated Mexican
National League. He was manager and part owner of the El Paso team. He finished his playing career right where it started — in El Paso.

As a player and player-manager, Andy showed such leadership qualities that it foregone conclusion that he
be in demand strictly as a manager when his playing days were over. He served successfully in that categorty
Denver, New Orleans and Indioapollos 
When the story broke that he had been appointed manager of the Denver Bears, I hastened to look him up. I found him selling
neckties at Al's Shop for Men. Few knew Andy was a haberdasher man. Maybe that's when he got his habit, of wearing colorful but stylish ties.

in his more mature years, Andy was a scout for the Boston Brave! id Philadelphia Phils. He was also in demand as a coach and instructor for 
the El Paso Sun Kings and El Paso Diablos.

For 17 years he was the varsity baseball coach at UT El Paso, a job that paid very little. Because UTEP put baseball on its athletic curriculum only to
fill out a requirement for the Western Athletic Conference and NCAA, there was hardly any money for recruiting.

Andy has a record that no big league manager can claim. In 1960 he was a coach on the Philadelphia Phillies’ staff. The manager resigned the day before the season opened. Andy
was named interim manager, a job that last one game. Andy managed the Phils to a 5-4 victory over Milwaukee in 10 innings. As a big league manager, Andy batted 1.000.

He played in the days when salaries didn't come close to the million-dollar figures of today. There were no free
agents or Te agents. You were bound to one club for the rest of your career. You could either sreept what was offered
or you quit. There was no arbitration. Holdouts could haggle, but they didn’t have the recourses they do today.
Andy looks back at his illustrlous career and says, “I don’t have any complaints.”


