ball star and athletic director.
“He was a great guy and a great friend of UTEP baseball and a great friend to me. I never heard him say a negative word.”
Cohen founded UTEP’s baseball program in 1960 and coached the team until he retired in 1976.
“He put El Paso baseball on the map and brought a lot of recognition and respect to El Paso,” said Jim Paul, owner of
the El Paso Diablos baseball team and a fellow inductee at Tuesday’s city Baseball Hall of Fame banquet
Paul said he had heard Cohen couldn’t attend the banquet because of failing health.
“Andy went off and made a name for himself in baseball and was gracious enough to come back and work with the El Paso Texans minor league
baseball team (1945-47) and later the El] Paso Sun Kings (in the 1960s).
“And he also worked with our Diablos on a volunteer basis.”
Paul said, “There will be a lot of people saddened by his death, Whenever I go to a World Series or a winter baseball meeting, people ask me how he’s doing.”
Paul said the key to Cohen’s popularity was that “he was a very positive person, never negative. He always had a 
smile. That’s a quality you don’t find in people anymore.
“I remember when he came out to a game in August, in his wheelchair. His mind was just as sharp as ever,” Paul said.
“In the fourth inning, he’d already done an analysis on our pitcher. And he was right.”
Cohen had been in a wheelchair since he had a stroke in 1982.
Karen Paul, Diablos business manager and Jim Paul’s sister, said Cohen took one last road trip by bus with the team
in 1979, when he was coaching first base for the Diablos.
“They went to Amarillo, and when they got back, the players said his stories made the time go by real fast. They
really liked him.
“Even after he was in a wheelchair, he was just as happy. He would cheer the team on and say, “The boys can 
do it. They’ll win.’”
Cohen made his mark in El Paso sports even before his professional career, when he Ted the El Paso High School
basketball team to a state championship in 1922.
“To me, he was a true hero,” said Jack Eisenberg, who headed the campaign to raise the money for the Cohen sports
complex. “He was the best you could expect from a sports hero. Donations came in from all over during the campaign,
from people who knew Andy or knew about him.”
Cohen’s Jewish background made many sports headlines in the 1920s. When he was called up to the Giants from Waco in
the old Texas League he was wined and dined by the New York Jewish community.
After three years with the Giants, Cohen played and coached for several minor league teams from New York
to Arkansas to Colorado.
In 1960 he was interim manager of the major league Philadelphia Phillies for one game, a winning effort that 
allowed him to joke later about how he may have been the only major league manager with a perfect record.
In 1955, he was the first El Pasoan elected to the El Paso Athletic Hall of Fame.
Most of his El Paso coaching was done for free, Jim Paul said.
Cohen worked as a sales representative for the Coors beer distributorship in El Paso from 1959 to 1972.
Much in demand as a public speaker and often the object of testimonials and honors, he was involved in many civic
activities, including the Lions Club, American Legion, Jewish War Veterans, and the Beautify El Paso Association.
He was an Army first sargeant in World War II, serving in North Africa and Italy.
Cohen was born in_Baltimore and grew up in El Paso.
